  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nIt will continue to be a pleasant late afternoon and evening \nacross the region with temperatures quickly cooling after \nsunset. Cirrus will gradually increase from south to north \ntonight from a nor'easter passing to the SE of Cape Cod. Given \nthat cirrus typically does not have a big impact on radiational \ncooling and that dew points are low, went ahead and lowered NBM \nforecast lows by a few degrees. This cools some interior valleys\ninto the 20s/lower 30s with middle to upper 30s elsewhere. The \nwarmest readings will be along the coast as mixing will likely \nlinger longer into the night. \n\nHigh pressure will remain over southern Quebec on Thursday into\nThursday night as a dry cold front crosses from the north and \nlow pressure passes well offshore. Partly to mostly sunny skies\nwill prevail with highs into the lower 60s south of the mtns \nwith 50s across the north. The increasing pressure gradient \nforce from the offshore low and high centered to our NW will \nallow for increasing winds throughout the day with N gusts up to\n30-35 mph likely from Thursday afternoon through the first half\nof Friday.\n\nUpper low/nor'easter will continue to move northeast, well out to \nsea. With persistent northerly flow aloft, expect a chilly and \nblustery day on Friday with highs mainly in the mid 40s to mid 50s. \nThe gusty winds will make it feel even colder. \n\nAs winds diminish Friday night, a mostly clear sky should prove \noptimal for radiational cooling and very cold temperatures. The \nlatest NBM is advertising lows in the 20s across the entire forecast \narea and this may end up being on the warm side depending on the \npresence of high clouds.  \n\nHigh pressure then dominates the weather pattern through the weekend \nas an upper level ridge nudges into the region. This will lead to a \ngradual warmup through the weekend and into next week. Highs on \nSaturday should mainly be back in the 50s with some 60s returning \nSunday and Monday. A cold front will sweep through on Monday which \nwill lead to cooler temperatures into midweek. As of now, this cold \nfront looks to be dry but we could end up seeing some low \nprecipitation chances as we move closer in time.",
